WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.660
John Wick might be known as&nbsp;
the Baba Yaga to his foes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.680
but did you know that the name has a&nbsp;
deep meaning rooted in Russian folklore?

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.540
Not all boogeymen are created equal. While many&nbsp;
action heroes have been revered by the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:18.060
of their fictional realities, few seem to inspire&nbsp;
the kind of dread and awe that Keanu Reeves' John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.100
Wick does. An utter legend of the underworld,&nbsp;
the well-known assassin and hitman came out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.668
of retirement in his original outing and has&nbsp;
been shedding blood relentlessly ever since.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.388
"But, now, yeah, I'm thinking I'm back."

00:00:31.388 --> 00:00:35.820
One of the most notable characteristics of&nbsp;
John Wick, however, is his unique nickname.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.620
In the vicious circles that the character&nbsp;
travels through, he is referred to as Baba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.820
Yaga. While many may be unaware of its&nbsp;
origins, particularly in North America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.860
denizens of Europe will likely be more familiar&nbsp;
with the folklore behind the foreboding name.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.180
A creature of Russian folklore, Baba&nbsp;
Yaga is something of a Slavic boogeyman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.760
or boogeywoman as the case might be. Like many&nbsp;
societies, the Slavic peoples told tales of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.680
frightening supernatural force that would come to&nbsp;
punish children if they behaved badly or wandered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.060
off alone. Though most cultures throughout&nbsp;
history have a similar figure, like Spain's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:15.241
Sack Man or Brazil's Cuca, part of what makes Baba&nbsp;
Yaga so scary is how downright strange she is.

00:01:15.458 --> 00:01:19.560
Though John Wick might be terrifying in his&nbsp;
own right, he's got nothing on the bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.640
and nightmarish stories of Baba Yaga. The&nbsp;
witch is said to keep her collection of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.600
human skulls on a fence as a warning to&nbsp;
any unlucky enough to stumble upon her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.060
lair. And if she's looking for you, she has no&nbsp;
shortage of terrifying ways to track you down.

00:01:33.241 --> 00:01:37.561
Baba Yaga has a flying kettle that she&nbsp;
can use to stalk her victims from above,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.561 --> 00:01:42.300
and she is also said to have a flying mortar&nbsp;
that she can pilot with a magical pestle. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.200
you think that's as weird as it gets, then&nbsp;
hold on to your flying kettles because Baba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.520
Yaga also lives in a hut that can run&nbsp;
around the woods on giant chicken legs.

00:01:50.773 --> 00:01:55.260
While that might sound absolutely bonkers,&nbsp;
you have to admit that the prospect of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.700
chased through the forest by a cottage on&nbsp;
bird legs is pretty frightening. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.300
there are less outwardly strange&nbsp;
aspects of Baba Yaga that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.478
help to explain why John Wick is known&nbsp;
by this name in the criminal underworld.

00:02:07.478 --> 00:02:11.820
One thing we all know about John Wick is that&nbsp;
when he's out for blood, a lot of people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.680
about to die. Being that Baba Yaga is said&nbsp;
to regularly join Death on his travels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.580
this adds context to why the assassin&nbsp;
has been branded with this moniker.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.700
In fact, the witch's reason&nbsp;
for following Death is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.980
that she can devour the souls of the newly dead.

00:02:31.364 --> 00:02:32.400
"Is that a child?"

00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:35.053
"It's just a human child."

00:02:35.053 --> 00:02:40.020
Many of us today aren't as worried today about&nbsp;
what happens to us after death. But in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.560
the prospect of not being allowed&nbsp;
to move on to the afterlife was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.960
a very real terror for people of many cultures.

00:02:46.358 --> 00:02:51.000
Terms like "the last rites" or a "Christian&nbsp;
burial" are still known to us to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.362 --> 00:02:55.500
There are similar beliefs from older cultures&nbsp;
that suggest that if a person is not properly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.240
laid to rest, they may wander forever&nbsp;
or even risk damnation. The fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.840
Baba Yaga doesn't just arrive with&nbsp;
death but seems to erase you from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.140
existence could add to the reasoning&nbsp;
behind John Wick's nickname as well.

00:03:07.598 --> 00:03:11.940
While most tales of Baba Yaga see her as&nbsp;
a mysterious and capricious villain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:16.980
a terrifying supernatural force, she is known&nbsp;
to appear in other forms and even help humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.780
from time to time. However, since Baba Yaga is&nbsp;
said to have two sisters who share her name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.620
it's possible that it is these versions&nbsp;
of the mythological figure that the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:28.346
fortunate characters have&nbsp;
run into in such stories.

00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:33.300
Like Loki of Norse mythology, Baba&nbsp;
Yaga can be seen as a friend or a foe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.960
depending on the circumstances. While&nbsp;
enemies of John Wick might see him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.340
a dangerous force to be reckoned with, Wick&nbsp;
also has a moral code and is likely to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.840
someone he cares for or relates to, despite&nbsp;
his knack for kill-or-be-killed brutality.

00:03:46.274 --> 00:03:50.400
Bedtime tales of John Wick as the Baba Yaga&nbsp;
could be at an end following "John Wick:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.720
Chapter 4." However, fans can still hold out&nbsp;
hope that his name will continue to haunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.200
them in more movies like the immortal&nbsp;
folktale figure for which he's named.

